  "49187456-2fc1-4d12-9e78-aae6566e691f": {
   "id": "49187456-2fc1-4d12-9e78-aae6566e691f",
   "message": {
    "id": "49187456-2fc1-4d12-9e78-aae6566e691f",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1766017268.387,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "**Scaled to 10 servings (×2.5):**\n- Chickpeas: 5 cans\n- Coconut milk: 2.5 cans (use 2 full-fat + 1 light, or 3 if you like it richer)\n- Onions: 2–3\n- Garlic: 7–8 cloves\n- Curry powder: 2.5 tbsp (taste; spices don't always need full linear scaling, so start with 2 and add)\n- Diced tomatoes: 2.5 cans (2 cans + a big spoon of tomato paste works)\n- Spinach: 5 cups, added at the end\n\nUse your biggest pot; brown onions in two batches if the pot is crowded or they'll steam.\n\n**Freezing:** coconut-milk curries freeze fine for 2–3 months. The sauce can separate a little on thawing; stir well while reheating and it comes back. Freeze **without** the spinach if possible and add fresh when reheating; frozen spinach in curry goes slimy but is still edible."
     ]
    },
